package murmur:task-io@0.1.0;

/// Host-provided capability available to any hook component.
///
/// A hook that imports this interface can read the text of the task its capsule
/// was handed and the result text the agent loop produced for it, without
/// holding a `filesystem` grant and without either payload being copied into a
/// lifecycle record. Nothing is copied into a hook's memory until the hook asks
/// for a window, so a hook that never imports this interface is never charged
/// for the bytes.
///
/// Reading is gated by `capabilities.task_io.read: true` on that hook's entry in
/// the capsule operator's own `murmur.yaml`. Default-deny: an ungranted hook
/// still links and still runs, and every function here returns `not-granted`.
///
/// # Timing
///
/// A task is *in scope* from the moment one of its attempts enters the agent
/// loop until the runtime's reopen loop returns for that task. What each
/// lifecycle event therefore sees:
///
/// | Lifecycle event | `input-*` | `output-*` |
/// |---|---|---|
/// | `on-stage` | `no-task` | `no-task` |
/// | `on-session-start` | `no-task` | `no-task` |
/// | `on-task-start` | `no-task` | `no-task` |
/// | `on-inference`, `on-tool-call`, `on-shell`, `on-compaction` | this attempt's input | `no-output` until the loop reaches a terminal arm |
/// | `on-task-end` | this attempt's input | this attempt's result text, or `no-output` if it ended without one |
/// | `on-session-end` | `no-task` | `no-task` |
///
/// `on-task-start` fires immediately *before* the task enters the reopen loop,
/// so the task is not yet in scope there. `on-session-end` fires once per launch
/// after the task loop, by which time the last task has left scope — an archiver
/// binds to `on-task-end`, not `on-session-end`.
///
/// On the second attempt of a reopened task the output is cleared at attempt
/// start, so a hook never sees attempt N-1's result while judging attempt N.
///
/// An `execution_mode: async` hook reads whatever is in scope **when its worker
/// reaches the call**, which may be after the task has left scope. This
/// interface is meant for blocking hooks; `reopen-task` is only honored from a
/// blocking hook anyway. Not enforced.
interface read {
    /// Which rendering of the task input to read.
    enum task-input-form {
        /// The exact text this attempt's agent loop was handed — what the model
        /// actually saw, including any reopen feedback appended by an earlier
        /// attempt. What an output gate should judge against.
        as-given,
        /// The task text before any reopen feedback was appended. What an
        /// archiver or cost-attribution hook wants. Byte-identical to `as-given`
        /// on a task that has never been reopened.
        original,
    }

    /// Why a read produced nothing.
    enum io-error {
        /// This hook's entry does not declare `capabilities.task_io.read: true`.
        /// A value to branch on, never a trap: the session continues.
        not-granted,
        /// No task is in scope at this lifecycle event — see the timing table.
        no-task,
        /// A task is in scope but its agent loop has not produced result text.
        /// Either the loop has not reached a terminal arm yet, or it ended on a
        /// path that returned an error without producing any.
        no-output,
        /// `offset` is past the end of the value, or lands inside a multi-byte
        /// UTF-8 character.
        out-of-range,
    }

    /// Byte length of the task input in `form`. Ask this first, then read the
    /// window you can afford — the host guesses no truncation cap.
    input-len: func(form: task-input-form) -> result<u64, io-error>;

    /// The longest prefix of the input's `[offset..]` that fits in `max-bytes`
    /// and ends on a UTF-8 character boundary, so a multi-byte character is
    /// never split.
    ///
    /// Advance `offset` by the byte length of what you got back to read the
    /// next window. An empty return with `offset` still below `input-len` means
    /// `max-bytes` was too small for the next character. `max-bytes: 0` returns
    /// an empty string; `offset` equal to the length returns an empty string.
    read-input: func(form: task-input-form, offset: u64, max-bytes: u64) -> result<string, io-error>;

    /// Byte length of the result text the agent loop produced for this attempt.
    output-len: func() -> result<u64, io-error>;

    /// The output's counterpart to `read-input`, with the same windowing and
    /// character-boundary rules.
    read-output: func(offset: u64, max-bytes: u64) -> result<string, io-error>;
}
